    },
    "Thank your pup for alerting you, then redirect with a 'quiet' cue or toy." : {

    },
    "Thanks for sharing!" : {

    },
    "The '5-minute rule' — puppies can walk 5 minutes per month of age per session." : {

    },
    "The 1-minute rule" : {

    },
    "The 3 D's: Always reduce one D when increasing another (e.g., shorter duration when adding distance)" : {

    },
    "The 3 D's: Duration, Distance, Distraction - only increase one at a time" : {
      "comment" : "Tip for the Training module on how to improve a skill by only increasing one of the three factors: duration, distance, or distraction.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated" : true
    },
    "The app learns from patterns and predicts when your puppy needs to pee." : {

    },
    "The Beginning" : {

    },
    "The click is a marker signal that tells your puppy exactly when they did something right. It bridges the gap between the behavior and the reward. Timing is everything — you must click at the precise moment the correct behavior happens, not a second before or after." : {

    },
    "The click marks the exact moment of correct behavior" : {

    },
    "The click marks the exact moment of correct behavior. Always reward within 1-2 seconds of clicking." : {

    },
    "The click must ALWAYS be followed by a treat - this is a fundamental rule" : {
      "comment" : "Tip for using the clicker correctly.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated" : true
    },
    "The click signals 'well done, you can relax now'. Don't expect the dog to hold position after clicking." : {

    },
    "The clicker is a marker signal that tells your puppy exactly when they did something right. It bridges the gap between the behavior and the reward." : {

    },
    "The crate gives you a timeout option and ensures your pup gets enough rest." : {

    },
    "The crate should always be positive. Never use it as punishment or your pup will resist going in." : {

    },
    "The data could not be read. Try restarting the app." : {

    },
    "The easiest and most useful command. A puppy who sits can't jump up at the same time - it replaces unwanted behavior naturally. You'll use this dozens of times every day." : {

    },
    "The first 16 weeks are critical. Track exposures to people, animals, sounds, and environments to raise a confident dog." : {

    },
    "The first weeks with a puppy are the most important. Otis helps you track patterns and build good habits." : {

    },
    "The Goal: Calm Neutrality" : {

    },
    "The goal: walk past a playground without breaking stride." : {

    },
    "The journey begins" : {

    },
    "The magic word works because it's never 'polluted' by everyday use. Your dog's name and 'come' have been said thousands of times with no reward — they've learned to tune them out" : {
      "comment" : "Tip for the \"Come\" skill, emphasizing the importance of using the magic word sparingly and without everyday distractions.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated" : true
    },
    "The moment that matters most" : {
      "comment" : "Explanation of the importance of clicking at the exact moment of correct behavior in training.",
      "isCommentAutoGenerated" : true
    },
    "The moment their bottom touches the floor: mark ('yes!' or click) and give the treat. Repeat 5-8 times per session." : {

    },
    "The name will always be imported." : {

    },
    "The over-socialization trap" : {

    },
    "The puppy is exposed to something and nothing bad happens. She observes from a comfortable distance, maybe gets a treat for being calm, and you move on. That's the positive experience — the absence of stress." : {

    },
    "The routine is taking shape." : {

    },
    "The shaded area shows healthy weight range for your puppy's breed size. Regular weigh-ins help spot growth issues early." : {

    },
